  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A Rapid Response Fund (RRF) Program Responding to Emergency Needs of Disaster Affected Populations in South Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Conflicts, macroeconomic crisis, and climate shocks continue to drive severe food crisis, with famine threats likely to occur in most areas of South Sudan and is likely to continue over the years. Subnational and localized violence could intensify with the ongoing tension between cattle keepers and farmers in greater Equatoria, along with fighting between opposition and splinter groups, as well as non?signatory groups to the 2018 peace deal. Climate and violence shocks are likely to result in new displacements in addition to the currently displaced persons, further disrupting livelihoods and basic services. Additionally, economic crisis, shown by the currency depreciation, food inflation and income reduction affect the purchasing power of households with 2.8 million people in emergency and 87,000 people in catastrophe. IOM aims to facilitate programming intervening to the acute emergencies triggered by both man-made and natural hazards affecting the most vulnerable population across South Sudan under the states of the Greater Equatorias (Western, Central and Eastern), Greater Upper Nile (Unity, Upper Nile and Jonglei) Greater Bahr el Ghazel (Lakes, Warrap, Western and Northern Bahr el Ghazel) and Abyei Administration Area.</narrative>
  </description>
